    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the number of different settings for a suitcase lock with three dials where each digit must be different, we can use the principle of counting permutations. The principle of counting permutations states that if there are \\( n \\) distinct objects and we want to arrange \\( r \\) of them, the number of ways to do this is given by \\( P(n, r) = \\frac{n!}{(n-r)!} \\).\n\nIn this case, we have 10 possible digits (0 through 9) and we need to arrange 3 of these digits in the three dials. Therefore, we need to calculate \\( P(10, 3) \\):\n\n\\[\nP(10, 3) = \\frac{10!}{(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{7!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the factorials. Recall that \\( 10! = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7! \\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10!}{7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{7!} = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8\n\\]\n\nNow, we just need to multiply these numbers together:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n90 \\times 8 = 720\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different settings possible is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{720}\n\\]",
